WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.940
- The final guest video in this run
is from Alie and Micah,

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:06.720
a neuroscientist and a clinical therapist
who run Neuro Transmissions.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:10.410
Their video has giant magnets, 3D printing,
and a Star Wars action figure.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.370
Alie, Micah, it's all yours.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.430
- We're here at the Keck Center
at UC San Diego.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:21.140
And this is a functional magnetic
resonance imager, or an fMRI.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.600
- Well, functional, but not functioning.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.170
This is actually a dummy scanner

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.150
that's used for educational
and training purposes.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:31.410
An actual fMRI machine
houses a 3-Tesla magnet

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:36.260
that's more than 60,000 times more
powerful than the Earth's magnetic field.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:40.090
And over 3,000 times more powerful
than your average fridge magnet.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.300
If we were anywhere near the real deal,

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.020
well, we would put our camera
and our equipment at serious risk.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:49.740
- But why on earth would anyone
possibly need a magnet so powerful?

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.410
Well, some of the researchers here

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:54.630
at UCSD are using fMRI
to study the human brain

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:58.750
in ways that 30 years ago
just weren't even possible.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:02.100
- I'm Maggie, I'm a fourth-year
graduate student at UC San Diego.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.740
- My name is Stephanie Nelli,

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:07.460
I'm a sixth-year PhD student
in John Serences' lab.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.250
- We study mostly selective attention
and expectation and other cognitive factors

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.680
and how they influence
visual processing in humans.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.950
- My research is about really basic
visual perception.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.070
How do we make sense
of the world around us?

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.530
How do we choose, out of
the plethora of things

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:24.760
that are constantly accosting
our visual system,

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.840
how does your brain make sense
of the massive amount of information

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.350
constantly bombarding it?

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.000
fMRI itself was developed
not very long ago,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.930
it's a very new technique,
as far as human research goes.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.770
And it was developed in the '90s, I believe,
actually in Bell Labs.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:44.780
- fMRI is a particular specialty of MRI
called functional MRI,

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:47.820
so what that does is basically, instead of
measuring the difference

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.210
between particular tissues,
you're actually optimising

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:54.040
to detect the difference between
oxygenated vs. deoxygenated blood

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:55.270
in a person's brain.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:59.700
- For a long time, it was impossible
to look at the brain up close

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.673
without cracking open someone's skull.

00:02:01.673 --> 00:02:02.506
[Micah laughs]

00:02:02.506 --> 00:02:06.120
And even if you got a brain from someone
who's passed on, that's not all that useful

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.160
for understanding how it works.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.790
When Leonardo Da Vinci dissected
the human brain in the 16th century

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:15.510
it was with the intention
of finding the seat of the human soul.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.561
Spoiler alert, he didn't.

00:02:17.561 --> 00:02:19.050
- Unsurprisingly, the functions

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.350
of different brain areas
were essentially a mystery

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.660
well after the scientific revolution.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:26.630
But fMRI changed all that
by giving scientists

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:29.830
the ability to see the
brain working in real time,

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:32.740
track activities in
different brain regions,

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.620
and read your mind.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:39.820
- No, unfortunately not for us scientists,
an fMRI machine cannot read the mind.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:43.620
It can tell you basically where blood
is being transported to in the brain.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.670
So which parts of the brain are active.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:49.630
- An fMRI scan can tell you when someone
is doing some cognitive task,

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:52.230
what kind of patterns of
brain activity you see.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:55.560
So which areas are active, what information
are those areas representing.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.070
For instance, if you have someone look
at different images and you measure MRI

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:02.520
while they do that, then later if you have
that same person come in and you say,

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:03.740
okay, think about something.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:07.120
Now, based on that information,
I can guess what you were thinking about.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.240
- I could probably say
you're looking to the left

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.320
by looking at your neural activity

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:14.060
or I could say you're looking
at a horizontal line

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.960
by looking at your neural activity.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.570
However, am I gonna be able to tell
what you're daydreaming about

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:22.170
or who you're in love with
or something like that?

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:27.780
No, I don't think think that's gonna happen
in my lifetime but, you know,

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.260
I said it on camera so
we'll be able to... [laughs].

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.830
- It might not quite make sense
how a magnet does all of this,

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.210
so let's break down the
process step by step.

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:39.820
Let's say that Rey here has been having

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:45.700
some neurologically linked problems
and a doctor refers her for an fMRI.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.191
All of her Force powers
are messing with her head.

00:03:49.191 --> 00:03:53.600
"Oh no, Kylo Ren keeps
appearing in my mind!

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.493
"I can't stop thinking about his hot torso.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:58.800
"Ohhh!"

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:01.980
- The technicians place her on the table,

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.880
give her some earplugs
and stabilise her head

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:06.230
so she can't move it at all.

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:09.510
If the head moves during the scan,
the images will come out fuzzy.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:12.830
Next, the table slides into
this large, doughnut-shaped

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:15.880
section which houses the
ultra-powerful magnet.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:17.810
The strong magnetic field of this magnet

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:22.490
then actually turns the
hydrogen atoms in our blood.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:23.900
- Wait, what?

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:28.060
- Yeah, the human body has a lot
of hydrogen atoms because well,

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:31.200
we're mostly made up of H₂O, that's water.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.450
The magnetic field from the fMRI interacts
with the protons in the hydrogen atoms

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:39.360
and makes those protons essentially point
in the same direction.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.380
That's right, your molecules are magnetic.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.490
Hence, the magnetic resonance part

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:46.530
of magnetic resonance imaging.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:47.793
- Once Rey's in position,
she'll hear a series

00:04:47.793 --> 00:04:50.844
of very loud clangs and beeps.

00:04:50.844 --> 00:04:58.100
[MRI machine beeps repeatedly]

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:00.710
But these sounds aren't
just the hottest new beat,

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:02.320
they actually serve a purpose.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:06.120
Every clang you hear is a radio wave pulse
being fired off.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:10.500
This radio wave disrupts the uniform direction
of the protons and pushes them

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.770
in slightly different directions.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:13.960
Here's the cool part.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.510
As the protons move back into realignment,

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:19.800
they release their very
own small radio signal

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.570
and those signals are then detected

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:24.380
by the fMRI machine's radio receiver,

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:27.870
which starts taking snapshots
of cross-sections of your brain,

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:29.640
which you hear as the beeping sound.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:33.470
After some complicated computation,
what shows up on the computer screen

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:35.360
is a series of images that show both

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.960
the anatomy of your brain
and highlighted areas

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.700
where there's more blood flow.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:41.580
Pretty cool, right?

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:46.430
- Yeah, so fMRI doesn't actually measure
your brain activity.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:49.270
It can't detect your
individual neurons firing,

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:51.547
as cool as that would be.

00:05:51.547 --> 00:05:55.110
fMRI machines actually detect
what's called the BOLD signal.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:58.750
- So the BOLD signal stands for
blood-oxygen-level dependent signal

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:02.390
and it's basically an index
of how much oxygenated haemoglobin

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:05.303
is in a person's blood at a particular
point in their brain.

00:06:05.303 --> 00:06:10.260
- Neurons themselves don't keep a high store
of glucose hanging around

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:14.330
so that they can do their job
and so the blood vessels,

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:16.770
through these things called
astrocytes, actually,

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.550
help supply neurons with the glucose

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:21.730
and oxygen that they need
to do their signalling.

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:24.000
- When you have neural activity
that happens in your brain,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.330
a bunch of blood will rush to that area

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:30.700
and it turns out that oxygenated
and deoxygenated haemoglobin in your blood

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:34.360
have different magnetic properties.
So deoxygenated haemoglobin

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:38.580
will disrupt the magnetic field
more than oxygenated haemoglobin will

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:40.850
and it'll actually cause
a decrease in the signal.

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:44.330
So when you look at an fMRI image,
the higher the signal you have,

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:46.480
the more oxygen is in
your blood at that point.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:47.693
And so you actually can measure

00:06:47.693 --> 00:06:50.730
with the BOLD signal after
these neural events occur.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.850
That basically tells you how much energy
was consumed at that location.

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:55.920
- Now, it's not perfect.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:58.370
It can't detect changes instantaneously

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:02.080
or tell you exactly what kinds
of signals are being sent.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:04.010
- But even with its limitations,

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:07.720
it lets us better understand
which brain regions do what.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:13.260
Using fMRI, scientists have been able
to identify 180 distinct brain regions.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:16.230
Better understanding the roles
of different brain regions means

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:19.330
that doctors can use that information
to help treat and support patients

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:22.740
with neurological disorders
or brain injuries.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:26.860
fMRI technology is cool,
but both of us wanted more.

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:30.730
We wanted to get into the machine
and see what our own brains looked like.

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:34.430
Maggie and Steph asked me to participate
in a pilot research study looking

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:38.650
at how humans distinguish between faces
and I jumped at the chance to help out.

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:43.370
- I didn't participate in the study
but I was able to get a structural scan.

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:45.040
It doesn't measure blood flow

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:48.690
but it does give you a high resolution image
of your brain's anatomy.

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:51.370
It was a strange experience, being inside.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.280
It felt like being in a plastic coffin.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:56.470
I can see why some people
get claustrophobic.

00:07:56.470 --> 00:08:00.550
- And participating in the study
was harder than I expected.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:02.540
While the task I was doing was easy,

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:04.560
it was hard to stay focused on doing

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.150
the same thing over and over
for almost two hours.

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:12.325
But it was also really
cool to see the results.

00:08:12.325 --> 00:08:15.870
- And yet, we could not pass up
on the opportunity of a lifetime.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.800
- I mean, sure it's cool to see your brain
on a screen

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:22.040
but imagine
holding it in your hands.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:26.210
What does my own unique brain
really look like?

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:29.650
Well, as it turns out,
we're able to find out.

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:33.460
- Thanks to modern software, we're able
to take the 2D slices of our brains

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:36.050
and compose them into a 3D render

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:37.600
and print them out.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:39.371
Cue the time lapse.

00:08:55.771 --> 00:08:58.600
- Well, look at that beautiful brain.

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.764
- I really liked how the rainbow filament
turned out on this.

00:09:01.764 --> 00:09:03.520
- Yeah, it's so pretty.
- This turned out really cool.

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:06.750
- I know, and I really like
my glow-in-the-dark print.

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:08.340
It's super bright.

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:11.920
- The grooves are much wider in yours,
and not quite as tightly packed,

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:14.620
whereas mine is super tightly folded.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.570
- It's cool that you
can see the inside too,

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:19.960
the hippocampus and all these
different brain regions.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:24.640
- Obviously I knew that my brain wasn't
perfectly symmetrical, no one's brain is,

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:28.220
but you can really see
very obvious differences.

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:32.530
It's really interesting to see,
like, oh, that's my brain's shape.

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:35.310
- Right, I didn't really
think about that before.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:37.180
- Every time someone
comes over to our house

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:39.082
I'll be like, did you see my brain?

00:09:39.082 --> 00:09:40.748
- Lemme show you my brain.
- Lemme show you my brain!

00:09:40.748 --> 00:09:43.930
- It's incredible that we have
the technology to track the activity

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:45.370
of a live human brain.

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:48.310
fMRI has pushed
neuroscience to new heights

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:51.680
and has given us a glimpse into the seat
of human consciousness.

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:53.890
And only somewhat less incredible

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:55.990
is the fact that we can
use those same images

00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:59.290
to build 3D models so we can
create a life-size version

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:00.870
of our own brains.

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:04.190
How funny that just a chunk
of squishy cells this big

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:05.880
can come up with such incredible tools

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:08.980
to help us better understand
our minds and ourselves.

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:12.850
- Huge thanks to Maggie and Steph
from the Serences lab for their expertise

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:16.330
and to UCSD for giving us
access to their scanner.

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:21.350
And thanks to Tom for giving nerds like us
the chance to share our brainy love.

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:22.893
Maybe we'll see you later?

00:10:22.893 --> 00:10:23.830
- Maybe?

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:26.470
- Until next time, we're
Neuro Transmissions,

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:27.830
over and out.

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:29.770
- Thank you folks, go subscribe
to Neuro Transmissions.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:32.100
I would recommend starting
with Micah's video

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:33.650
on training a cat to high five

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:36.380
or Alie's video on how
marijuana affects your brain.

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:38.090
And that's it! I'm back next week

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:39.473
and I will see you then.

